course: dictionary
lesson: 2029
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around the word, tells you which meaning is right. Confirmation can mean a message that proves something is true, or a church ceremony.
explain: «You will get a booking confirmation by email.» Here confirmation is a message that proves your booking is definitely made. Now read: «In confirmation a person confirms for themselves the promises made at their baptism.» This time it is a ceremony, and the words baptism and promises are the clues.
explain: Look at the verb hidden inside: to confirm means to make sure something is true, or to say yes again. Test your meaning by putting «proof» or «ceremony» into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Confirmation means a message or proof that something is true or definitely happening.
fact: In church, confirmation is a Christian ceremony where a young person or adult confirms their faith for themselves.
fact: In a confirmation ceremony, a bishop often lays hands on the person and prays.
fact: «Confirmation bias» means remembering the times that fit your idea and forgetting the times that don't.
fact: In science, an experiment that matches its prediction gives confirmation.
exercise: «You will get a booking confirmation by email.» What is a booking confirmation? | A picture of a hotel | A bus ticket you keep in your pocket | A letter asking you to cancel | *A message that proves your booking is definitely made
exercise: «In confirmation a person confirms for themselves the promises made at their baptism.» In this sentence, what is confirmation? | A shop sign | *A church ceremony | A type of car | A kind of weather
exercise: «Remembering the times that fit your idea and forgetting the times that don't is called confirmation bias.» What does confirmation bias mean? | *Remembering only what matches what you believe | Buying things you do not need | Checking the weather before you go out | Forgetting every idea you ever had
test: «The experiment matched the prediction, which gave the scientist confirmation.» What does confirmation mean here? | A cake recipe | A bright light in the lab | *Proof that the result is true
test: «A bishop laid his hands on the young people during their confirmation.» Which clue tells you confirmation is a ceremony and not a message? | Young | *Bishop | Hands | Day
exam: Lily reads «Your order is confirmed» and «She had her confirmation last spring.» Why does confirmation mean something different in each sentence? | Because Lily guessed both meanings | *The context: an order needs proof, and the church words show a ceremony | Because the two sentences are spelled differently | Because confirmation always means the same thing
